#: A detached window on a desktop: dragged, resized, zoomed and raised.
#:
#: Turbo Vision's ``TWindow``, and what DOS Navigator's file panels, viewer
#: and editor all are.  It lives in a :class:`~navml.widgets.desktop.Desktop`,
#: which owns the z-order and says which window is active; the window owns
#: its own rectangle and the chrome that changes it.
#:
#: **Nothing about the geometry is written here, and that is the rule this
#: component exists under.**  A drag assigns ``x`` and ``y``, a resize assigns
#: ``width`` and ``height``, a zoom assigns all four -- and a bound attribute
#: is read-only until something unbinds it.  So the rectangle is *state*, like
#: a panel's ``path``: a derived document may give it a starting value with a
#: literal line, which is an assignment and not a binding, and may never bind
#: it.  See *A property a widget navigates cannot be bound* in DESIGN.md.
Window:
    #: Shown centred on the top edge of a framed window.
    property title: ""

    #: The window's place in Win+1 .. Win+9 and the window strip, given by
    #: the desktop when the window opens: the lowest of 1 .. 9 no other
    #: window holds, kept while windows are raised and lowered, and moved
    #: down when one before it closes (``Desktop._renumber``).  Turbo
    #: Vision's ``TWindow.Number``; 0 is its ``wnNoNumber``, a tenth window's.
    property number: 0

    #: Whether the ``[■]`` icon is painted, and answers a click.
    property closable: True

    #: The control the keyboard goes to first, or None for the first one in
    #: the tab order -- ``first_focus: tree``.  Asked whenever the keyboard is
    #: handed in; one that is hidden or cannot take it is passed over.
    #: Read by :meth:`take_keyboard` on every activation, never from
    #: ``mounted()``: that runs inside ``Desktop.open``'s ``add()``, before
    #: ``activate`` has saved which panel the window below had, so closing
    #: this one would hand the keyboard to the left panel.
    property first_focus: None

    #: The menu a right click or Shift+F10 opens over the window, or None for
    #: none: usually its own menu on the bar -- ``local_menu: volumes_menu``
    #: -- so the two are one list.  A departure, as every context menu is.
    property local_menu: None

    #: Whether the ``[↑]`` icon is painted, and the title answers a double
    #: click.
    property zoomable: True

    #: Whether the bottom-right corner is a grip.
    property resizable: True

    #: Whether Window > Tile and Cascade arrange it.  Turbo Vision's
    #: ``ofTileable``, but **on by default**, where Turbo Vision had it off and
    #: DOS Navigator set it on only some of its windows (not the tree window):
    #: every window on a desktop is free-floating and takes part.  Kept as the
    #: way out for a window that must stay where it is.
    property tileable: True

    #: The smallest a resize may make it.  Turbo Vision's ``minWinSize``.
    property min_width: 16
    property min_height: 6

    #: Filling the whole desktop, with the rectangle it came from kept aside.
    #: Declared here so that a derived document can open zoomed with one
    #: literal line; toggled by :meth:`toggle_zoom`, never bound.
    property zoomed: False
